{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Ms. ___. I’m Dr. ___ and I’ll be taking care of you tonight. You came in because you were short of breath. Can you tell me more about that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, doctor. I’ve been feeling worse for about two days. I walked to the bathroom without my oxygen and started gasping. I’ve also gained some weight lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You’re supposed to be on 2 liters of oxygen, right? And you haven’t been using it consistently?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Well… no. It’s hard to carry around. And sometimes I forget. Plus, I haven’t been taking all my pills either."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s important to know. Your breathing trouble is likely from your COPD and heart failure. Not using oxygen or your medications can make it worse. We’ve started you on nebulizers and a steroid pill to help your lungs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that help me breathe better?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and we’re giving you oxygen now. You should feel improvement soon. We also checked your heart with blood tests and a chest X-ray. No signs of pneumonia or fluid in the lungs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:12:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My heart’s okay then?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your heart function looks stable. Your BNP level is 506, which is much lower than during past flare-ups. But your weight gain and diet may have made your breathing worse. You’ve been eating salty foods?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, I guess. The food at the nursing home isn’t great, but I sneak extra snacks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That can hold onto fluid and strain your heart. We’re keeping you on your 160 mg Lasix to help with that. Your kidney function is normal, so it’s safe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I stopped taking it because I was going to the bathroom too much."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand, but it’s important for your heart. We’ll work with the nursing home to help manage that. There’s one more thing—we noticed your EKG shows a longer-than-normal QT interval."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What does that mean?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It means there’s a risk for an irregular heartbeat. We stopped your fluoxetine and trazodone because they can make it worse. You shouldn’t take those anymore."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But those help me sleep and feel better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2132-06-11 18:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I know, but your heart rhythm is more important right now. We’ll talk to your psychiatrist about safer options. For now, we’re avoiding any medicines that could affect your QT."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You’re doing much better. Your breathing has improved, and your labs are stable. We’re going to discharge you today to the extended care facility."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. What do I need to do when I get back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Finish the 5-day prednisone course, use your albuterol inhaler as needed, and keep using oxygen—especially when walking. We’re sending one with you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll try."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And please take your Lasix every day. Weigh yourself daily—if you gain more than 3 pounds, call your nurse. Also, no salty foods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do my best."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "One last thing: get an EKG every day for the next week to check your QT. If it stays long, you’ll need to see a cardiologist."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you, doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Your daughter will be your contact. We’ll send all instructions to her and your care team."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "She’ll help me stay on track."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2132-06-12 09:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s great. Keep working with her. Take care, and we’ll see you in follow-up if needed."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2132-06-11 17:45:00",
    "admission_end": "2132-06-12 16:06:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of COPD, chronic diastolic heart failure, obesity hypoventilation syndrome, and obstructive sleep apnea was admitted for dyspnea after noncompliance with oxygen, medications, and diet. She presented with mild respiratory distress and was found to have stable cardiomegaly on chest X-ray and a low BNP, suggesting no acute heart failure exacerbation. Treatment included oxygen, nebulizers, and a prednisone burst for COPD exacerbation, while continuing home diuretics for volume management. A prolonged QTc on EKG led to discontinuation of fluoxetine and trazodone. Her symptoms improved rapidly, and she was discharged to extended care with instructions to complete steroids, use prescribed inhalers and oxygen, monitor weight, and obtain daily EKGs to assess QT interval.",
